---
// Motivation invites a community to examine capability across roles and domains.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../styles/home.css";
import "../styles/motivation.css";
import "../styles/toolbox.css";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const LENS_PROGRAM_URL =
  "https://education.jhu.edu/masters-programs/master-of-education-in-learning-design-and-technology/learning-engineering-next-generation-systems-concentration/";
const LECOMMONS_URL = "https://lecommons.org";
// One-page slick sheet, rendered from slick-sheet/lens-slick-sheet.html by
// scripts/build-slick-sheet.sh. Replaces the May 2026 white-paper overview.
// The two-page companion is deliberately not linked here yet — it lives
// unlisted at about/what-you-will-do/ until the program announces it.
const OVERVIEW_PDF_URL = base + "LENS_Overview_Aug2026.pdf";
---

<Base
  title="Motivation · Capability Matters"
  description="Why human capability belongs in system design. Explore the motivation for Capability Matters and the Johns Hopkins LENS pathway."
  active="motivation"
  pageTheme="warm"
>
  <link slot="head" rel="canonical" href="https://capabilitymatters.org/" />
  <link
    slot="head"
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Instrument+Serif:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,300;0,400;0,500;0,700;1,300;1,400&display=swap"
  />

  <div class="motivation-layout">
    <div class="motivation-main">
  <section class="hero" id="argument">
    <h1>System performance depends on <em>what people can do.</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-bridge motivation-purpose">Capability Matters brings people together around a shared question: how do we help people do work that matters? Explore cases, labs and design briefs, bring your experience, and help us test what works.</p>
    <p class="hero-bridge">Learning engineering connects learning sciences, human-centered design and evidence to improve capability. You don't need to call yourself a learning engineer to take part.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="community-invitation" aria-labelledby="community-title">
    <h2 id="community-title">There is a place for you here.</h2>
    <p>Whatever your role, your view of the work belongs in the conversation.</p>
    <ul class="community-roles">
      <li><h3>Learners &amp; students</h3><p>Bring your questions and experience of learning. Try a case or lab and tell us what helps.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Educators, trainers &amp; coaches</h3><p>Share what learners need, how you support them, and where learning breaks down.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Practitioners &amp; frontline teams</h3><p>Whether you care for patients, operate equipment, maintain systems or deliver services, bring the realities of the job.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Designers, engineers &amp; technologists</h3><p>Connect tools, workflows and learning. Make the assumptions in a design visible and testable.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Researchers &amp; evaluators</h3><p>Help us ask better questions, examine evidence and distinguish promising ideas from demonstrated results.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Leaders &amp; workforce partners</h3><p>Bring decisions about resources, readiness, opportunity and the conditions people need to succeed.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Families, communities &amp; public partners</h3><p>Help define what matters, who benefits and whose experience a system may overlook.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="community-next"><a href={base + "case-studies/"}>Explore a case together</a> · <a href={LECOMMONS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Connect with the learning engineering community</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="statement right">
    <h2>Human capability belongs <em>in the design.</em></h2>
    <p>
      Hardware gets reliability analysis. Software gets tested to specification. Human capability needs the same attention, from the first design decisions through operation and revision.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="statement">
    <h2>Capability is not a <em>soft</em> problem. It's a systems engineering problem.</h2>
    <p>
      How people develop, retain, and transfer operational competence is as tractable as any other engineering domain—when you bring the right methods, the right evidence, and the right integration into the system lifecycle.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section class="statement right">
    <h2>Decision-grade evidence. <em>Not opinions about training.</em></h2>
    <p>
      Can you prove your people are ready? Not with completion rates or satisfaction scores. With evidence that links learning interventions to operational outcomes—evidence rigorous enough to drive design decisions.
    </p>
  </section>

    </div>
  <aside class="motivation-stats" aria-labelledby="stats-title">
    <h2 id="stats-title">What is at stake</h2>
    <div class="evidence-cell">
      <div class="num">250K+</div>
      <div class="unit">estimated deaths per year</div>
      <p>estimated annual U.S. deaths attributed to medical error in a 2016 analysis. The authors call for better measurement and attention to system failures.</p>
      <div class="cite"><a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/353/bmj.i2139">Makary &amp; Daniel, <em>BMJ</em>, 2016</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-cell">
      <div class="num">~10yr</div>
      <div class="unit">of unmet training requirements</div>
      <p>GAO reported in 2024 that Marine Corps training ranges in the Indo-Pacific had been unable to meet all training requirements for almost a decade.</p>
      <div class="cite"><a href="https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-24-107463.pdf">GAO-24-107463, 2024 (PDF)</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-cell">
      <div class="num">$100M</div>
      <div class="unit">in funding; the initiative closed</div>
      <p>The inBloom initiative received $100 million in funding. Its closure raises questions about privacy, governance and public trust alongside technical design.</p>
      <div class="cite"><a href="https://datasociety.net/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/InBloom_feb_2017.pdf">Data &amp; Society, 2017 (PDF)</a></div>
    </div>
  </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="toolbox-feature" aria-labelledby="toolbox-heading">
    <div class="toolbox-feature-copy">
      <p class="toolbox-eyebrow">Learning engineering in practice</p>
      <h2 id="toolbox-heading">The right tools, <em>working together.</em></h2>
      <p>Learning engineering draws on a toolbox of complementary methods and expertise—from learning sciences and design to systems engineering, analytics, human–machine teaming, and domain expertise.</p>
      <p>No one practitioner has to master every tool. The important work happens at their interfaces: assembling the right people and methods around a real capability problem.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="toolbox-feature-figure">
      <img src={base + "learning-engineering-toolbox.svg"} width="1200" height="650" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="An open toolbox with nine tools for learning design; learning sciences and research; AI and emerging technology; data and learning analytics; human–machine teaming; systems engineering and design thinking; communication and leadership; ethics and compliance; and domain application." />
      <figcaption>Different competencies are tools for a shared purpose—not separate definitions of learning engineering.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="domains" id="domains" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-labelledby="domains-title">
    <div class="domains-header">
      <h2 id="domains-title">Find your domain. Bring your experience.</h2>
      <p>Use these resources to explore a question from your work. Browse the domains with the buttons, keyboard arrows or a swipe.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="domain-invitation">Your field may look different. Bring your own challenge and use what helps.</p>
    <div class="domain-controls" aria-label="Browse domains"><button type="button" data-domain-direction="-1" aria-controls="domain-carousel" aria-label="Previous domain">← Previous</button><span id="domain-position" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">1 of 7 · Healthcare</span><button type="button" data-domain-direction="1" aria-controls="domain-carousel" aria-label="Next domain">Next →</button></div>
    <div class="domain-cards" id="domain-carousel" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Domains; use left and right arrow keys to browse">
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⚕</div>
        <h3>Healthcare</h3>
        <p>Clinicians, patients, caregivers and care teams: examine alerts, handoffs and new protocols. Use a case to ask what safe, effective care requires.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⛨</div>
        <h3>Defense &amp; National Security</h3>
        <p>Operators, maintainers, analysts and program teams: map readiness, training and support. Explore which capability decisions belong early in a system’s life.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">◈</div>
        <h3>Education at Scale</h3>
        <p>Students, educators, families and learning designers: try learning activities and examine access, feedback and transfer. Bring the conditions your learners face.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">✈</div>
        <h3>Aviation</h3>
        <p>Pilots, controllers, engineers and maintenance crews: trace how automation, information and training interact. Compare decisions before a system enters service.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⚡</div>
        <h3>Energy</h3>
        <p>Grid, refinery and offshore teams, engineers and communities: explore preparedness, changing conditions and recovery. Ask what people need to notice and do.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⚙</div>
        <h3>Manufacturing &amp; Industrial</h3>
        <p>Production teams, technicians, supervisors and safety partners: try the Andon lab. Examine how signals, timely help and shared authority support improvement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="domain-card" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">⬡</div>
        <h3>Government &amp; Public Infrastructure</h3>
        <p>Residents, public servants, librarians and service teams: use cases and demos to examine access, trust and service delivery. Help define what useful participation looks like.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-section" id="connect">
    <div class="cta-equation">Human + System = Impact</div>
    <h2 >Human capability is a designable, measurable, <em>engineerable</em> property of every complex system.</h2>
    <p >LENS is a graduate specialization at Johns Hopkins that prepares practitioners to do this work—grounded in learning sciences and human systems integration, built for the domains where it matters most.</p>
    <div class="cta-buttons">
      <a href={LENS_PROGRAM_URL} class="cta-btn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore the LENS specialization</a>
      <a href={LECOMMONS_URL} class="cta-btn secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore the wider learning engineering field</a>
    </div>
    <p class="cta-tertiary">
      <a href={OVERVIEW_PDF_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download the one-page LENS overview (PDF)</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="capability-note" aria-labelledby="capability-note-title">
    <p class="capability-note-eyebrow">From Show Your Work · A community working paper</p>
    <h2 id="capability-note-title">What we mean by capability</h2>
    <p class="capability-note-definition">The capacity of a person, team, or human-system arrangement to accomplish valued work under stated conditions, with usable opportunities to act.</p>
    <div class="capability-note-footer">
      <a href={base + "approach/"}>Explore the Approach <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <p>William Gray-Roncal &amp; James Diamond · Working definition for community discussion</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>


<script>
  const carousel = document.getElementById('domain-carousel');
  const position = document.getElementById('domain-position');
  if (carousel && position) {
    const cards = Array.from(carousel.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.domain-card'));
    const current = () => cards.reduce((best, card, i) => Math.abs(card.offsetLeft - carousel.scrollLeft) < Math.abs(cards[best].offsetLeft - carousel.scrollLeft) ? i : best, 0);
    const move = (direction: number) => {
      const index = Math.max(0, Math.min(cards.length - 1, current() + direction));
      carousel.scrollTo({left: cards[index].offsetLeft, behavior: 'instant'});
    };
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-domain-direction]').forEach(button => {
      button.addEventListener('click', () => move(Number(button.dataset.domainDirection)));
    });
    carousel.addEventListener('keydown', event => {
      if (!['ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight'].includes(event.key)) return;
      event.preventDefault(); move(event.key === 'ArrowRight' ? 1 : -1);
    });
    const updatePosition = () => {
      const index = current();
      position.textContent = `${index + 1} of ${cards.length} · ${cards[index].querySelector('h3')?.textContent}`;
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-domain-direction]').forEach(button => {
        button.disabled = Number(button.dataset.domainDirection) < 0 ? index === 0 : index === cards.length - 1;
      });
    };
    carousel.addEventListener('scroll', updatePosition, {passive: true});
    updatePosition();
  }
</script>
